454
00:45:46,150 --> 00:45:58,069
And one of the reasons I started writing this up was because I had the impression that there were a lot of misconceptions about what failure is in C or what a fault is in C and things like that.

455
00:45:58,069 --> 00:46:06,710
So basically I was categorizing failures, program failures into four main groups.

456
00:46:06,710 --> 00:46:09,030
The first one is wrongdoings.

457
00:46:09,030 --> 00:46:11,990
So it's your fault when you do something wrong.

458
00:46:11,990 --> 00:46:18,390
I don't know you dreference a null pointer or you you do whatever bad thing you can do in C.

459
00:46:18,390 --> 00:46:21,750
This is basically the easy part where

460
00:46:21,750 --> 00:46:32,950
you should whenever it's possible get a diagnostic from your compiler or your program should really fault very quickly or something like that.

461
00:46:32,950 --> 00:46:44,710
Then there is the next layer of faults that lead to program failure which are program state degradation.

462
00:46:44,710 --> 00:46:48,790
So for example if you have store ex exhaustion.

463
00:46:48,800 --> 00:46:57,990
So this is generally something which you can check if you check for example the return of system calls.

464
00:46:57,990 --> 00:47:06,150
So if malo really gives you a null back then you know that something deeply went wrong with your program.

465
00:47:06,150 --> 00:47:16,631
there's no storage available anymore and so you really should stop and do an emergency C exit from your program or something like that. So that was the second level.

466
00:47:16,631 --> 00:47:20,790
The third level are unfortunate incidents.

467
00:47:20,800 --> 00:47:32,950
Unfortunate incidents are things that you don't really notice, you don't which are difficult to track and things like that.

468
00:47:32,950 --> 00:47:38,309
For example, like a race condition in between two threads in a in a threaded program.

469
00:47:38,309 --> 00:47:44,550
There are two entities doing their things and two threads for example and

470
00:47:44,550 --> 00:47:50,390
seen locally everybody's doing the right thing but together they don't work.

471
00:47:50,390 --> 00:47:58,069
This is something which you can basically only avoid by really careful design.

472
00:47:58,069 --> 00:48:06,790
So this false program failures for unfortunate incidents usually only appear when you have a bad design.

473
00:48:06,790 --> 00:48:11,430
But what is a good and what a bad design obviously is a really difficult question.

474
00:48:11,430 --> 00:48:22,390
And then you have the fourth thing which is a series of unfortunate incidents where you are basically caught in a bubble.

475
00:48:22,390 --> 00:48:24,630
You have doing decisions on your own.

476
00:48:24,630 --> 00:48:30,309
You're you're going around a block in a city for example,

477
00:48:30,309 --> 00:48:41,510
at each corner you decide because of something to go to the left and if you do that consistently you will always walk around the block and never get out.

478
00:48:41,510 --> 00:48:52,710
So you're caught in some sort of bubble because you're taking local decisions decisions which don't lead you which don't have you make progress at some point.

479
00:48:52,710 --> 00:48:55,510
So this is also a really difficult design question to answer.

480
00:48:55,510 --> 00:48:59,670
So this is what failure is.

481
00:48:59,670 --> 00:49:08,790
But the problem then is that you have to really distinguish the error.

482
00:49:08,790 --> 00:49:11,510
So if you talk about a sickness or something like that.

483
00:49:11,520 --> 00:49:18,390
So this would be something which is wrong or goes wrong from the way it manifests from the symptoms.

484
00:49:18,390 --> 00:49:26,549
And this is something which is often conflated by people in C and generally in programming languages I think.

485
00:49:26,549 --> 00:49:36,950
So so symptoms can be compiler diagnostics, there can be compilation failure, there can be program termination or even worse there can be real crashes.

486
00:49:36,950 --> 00:49:39,670
There can be erratic behavior, silent misfunctioning.

487
00:49:39,680 --> 00:49:45,750
All these different symptoms can appear if you have a failing program.

488
00:49:45,750 --> 00:49:52,790
You really have to navigate these and to know why your program fails and

489
00:49:52,790 --> 00:50:00,390
what to do against it to first of all try to do some to work that

490
00:50:00,390 --> 00:50:08,390
it never happens or if you can't avoid it then see how to manage if it occurs and terminate your program for example in a controlled way.

